# config/ghl-mcp-pin.env — SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the GHL Community MCP
# (Tier 2, skill 36) supply-chain pin and runtime tool profile.
#
# Sourced by:
#   scripts/ghl-mcp-autostart.sh
#   scripts/ghl-mcp-probe.sh
#   platform/vps/36-ghl-mcp-setup-scripts/start-ghl-mcp-server.sh
# Read (never sourced) by:
#   scripts/ghl-mcp-pin-digest.sh          (the vetting-digest primitive)
#   scripts/qc-assert-ghl-mcp-supervised.sh (static gate)
#   scripts/ghl-mcp-assert-runtime.sh       (runtime conformance gate)
#
# DELIVERED TO BOXES BY (v21.6.0 — before this, it was a REPO-ONLY document):
#   install.sh        -> $OC_CONFIG/onboarding/config/  AND  $OC_CONFIG/config/
#   update-skills.sh  -> $OC_CONFIG/config/
# where $OC_CONFIG is ~/.openclaw (Mac) or /data/.openclaw (VPS). Every
# resolver list in every consumer must stay a SUBSET of those destinations —
# scripts/qc-assert-pin-delivery-paths.sh asserts exactly that in CI, because
# the v21.5.0 release shipped this file to the repo and to no box at all.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   The pin used to live in THREE places that disagreed: INSTALL.md prose
#   (3dd9006a), the VPS overlay (3dd9006a short SHA), and the EXECUTED
#   autostart script (no pin at all — it ran `git pull --ff-only` / a
#   `--depth 1` clone of whatever `main` pointed at that minute). Upstream
#   force-pushes rewritten history, so a floating clone is supply-chain
#   roulette. One file, read by every launch surface, ends the drift.
#
# ✅ VETTING GATE — CLOSED FOR THE CURRENT PIN (bfc2bbe, verdict CLEAN, see
#    GHL_MCP_PIN_VETTED_* below). The gate stays a gate: DO NOT ROLL A **NEW**
#    PIN FLEET-WIDE UNTIL ITS OWN COMMIT-VETTING VERDICT IS **CLEAN**. The
#    installer refuses to start an un-pinned tree, but it cannot judge whether
#    the pinned tree is trustworthy — a human/agent commit review does that.
#    Any change to GHL_MCP_VETTED_COMMIT MUST reset the three
#    GHL_MCP_PIN_VETTED_* fields to PENDING until the new commit is re-vetted.
#
# HOW TO BUMP THE PIN: see "A LEGITIMATE PIN BUMP IS TWO COMMANDS" in the
# vetting-digest block below. Hand-editing any bound field leaves a stale digest
# and EVERY BOX REFUSES TO START (STATUS: PIN_UNVETTED). That refusal is the
# feature, not a bug.

# Full 40-char upstream commit. busybee3333/Go-High-Level-MCP-2026-Complete.
# bfc2bbe = "feat: add easy setup commands and CRM agent tools" (2026-06-11).
# This is the commit currently deployed and verified working on the operator
# box (src/main.ts DOES contain `await requestServer.connect(transport)` — the
# line whose absence in a STALE compiled dist/main.js made every agent init
# hang 30s for two days).
GHL_MCP_VETTED_COMMIT="32628c979dd8f46e7952f85ce2d98f8028a26c32"

# Vetting provenance. A verdict other than CLEAN blocks a fleet roll.
#
# VERDICT CLEAN — recorded 2026-08-03. The security review of the pinned tree at
# bfc2bbe covered the four things that could make a third-party MCP dangerous,
# and all four came back negative:
#   1. Credential layer — BYTE-IDENTICAL to the previously trusted tree. No new
#      code reads, stores, forwards or logs the PIT.
#   2. Outbound surface — NO new outbound hosts. The server talks only to the
#      GoHighLevel API hosts it already talked to.
#   3. Endpoint construction — all 245 generated endpoints verified to build
#      RELATIVE paths against the configured API base. None can be redirected to
#      an attacker-controlled origin by data.
#   4. Dependency graph — UNCHANGED. No added, removed or re-pointed packages,
#      so there is no new transitive supply-chain surface.
GHL_MCP_PIN_VETTED_ON="2026-08-03"
GHL_MCP_PIN_VETTED_BY="openclaw-onboarding commit-vetting review of mirror branch openclaw-patched: upstream bfc2bbe (unchanged, previously CLEAN) + openclaw-patch-01 loopback-bind/Origin-403/optional-bearer, authored downstream and built+tested (19 assertions) before sealing; dependency graph unchanged; new host literals are allow-list and test fixtures, not request targets"
GHL_MCP_PIN_VETTED_VERDICT="CLEAN"   # CLEAN | DIRTY | PENDING

# ── SELF-INVALIDATING VETTING DIGEST (v21.6.0) ───────────────────────────────
# The verdict above is now ENFORCED, not advisory: the installers REFUSE to
# build or start unless it reads CLEAN (STATUS: PIN_UNVETTED). But a verdict
# field alone is defeated by editing one word, so the real mechanism is the
# digest below — it binds the verdict to the commit. Change the commit by hand
# and the digest stops recomputing, and every consumer refuses. FORGETTING
# PRODUCES REFUSAL. That is the whole design.
#
# THE DIGEST IS NOT A SIGNATURE. It is a plain unkeyed SHA-256 over
#     ghl-mcp-pin-v2 | commit | verdict | on | by | deps_lock_sha256 | repo_url
# (see scripts/ghl-mcp-pin-digest.sh for the byte-exact definition — that
# script is the ONE canonical implementation; nothing may reimplement it).
# Anyone with repo write access can recompute it. It stops forgetting, not
# attackers. Upgrade path for tamper-evidence: sign the same tuple with a
# signed git tag or minisign; the consumer check shape is unchanged.
#
# The tuple's fifth field, GHL_MCP_DEPS_LOCK_SHA256, is declared further down
# (with the lockfile it pins and the command that recomputes it). It is NOT
# duplicated here: this file is `.`-sourced top-to-bottom, so a second
# assignment would silently shadow the first, and a digest computed over a
# shadowed value would be wrong in a way nothing would notice.
#
# ✅ SEALED. scripts/ghl-mcp-vet-pin.sh exists and wrote the digest below. The
# transitional fallback (an empty digest meaning "fall back to requiring
# VERDICT=CLEAN") is still implemented in the box-side installers on purpose —
# a box mid-roll may still be holding an older delivered pin file and must not
# be bricked by one — but it is no longer reachable from a repo-authored pin:
# scripts/ghl-mcp-check-pin-digest.sh treats an ABSENT digest as a FAILURE, so
# CI and the pre-push hook refuse to let an unsealed record be authored again.
#
# A LEGITIMATE PIN BUMP IS TWO COMMANDS, and hand-editing is not one of them:
#   1. REVIEW (read-only; prints credential layer / outbound hosts / endpoint
#      construction / dependency graph as a mechanical diff against the mirror):
#        bash scripts/ghl-mcp-vet-pin.sh <candidate-40-char-sha>
#   2. SEAL (rewrites this file's tuple in place and prints the next commands):
#        bash scripts/ghl-mcp-vet-pin.sh <candidate-40-char-sha> --verdict clean
# Reading the report is the only part that needs judgment. That is less work
# than the three-file hand-edit it replaced, which is the point: an enforcement
# mechanism that costs more than the thing it replaces gets disabled.
#
# Written ONLY by scripts/ghl-mcp-vet-pin.sh. NEVER hand-edit this value.
GHL_MCP_PIN_VETTED_DIGEST="9c5d9a33b30091ed9ad57089fd507ccff0000cbedfd34a5c792e7b2eace638d3"

# Runtime tool surface. Upstream default is `full` = the whole 858-tool
# catalogue; when the server is registered in mcp.servers that entire catalogue
# rides in EVERY agent's init. Valid values (src/tool-registry.ts:508-513):
#   curated | official | stable | raw | full
# `curated` is the smallest sane surface (the agent-workspace CRM workflow
# tools — 43 tools as measured on the operator box on 2026-08-03) and is what
# upstream's own editor-configure commands use (`--profile curated`).
GHL_MCP_TOOL_PROFILE="curated"

# ⚠️ PROFILE CONSEQUENCE — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED (2026-08-03, operator box):
#   `curated` exposes 43 tools and they are ALL the crm_* agent-workspace
#   workflow tools. The classic `ghl_*` tools are NOT in it — verified:
#   GET /tools under curated does not contain `ghl_list_products`.
#   Skill 36 documents Tier 2 as the path for products, invoices, billing,
#   subscriptions, estimates, store, coupons, Voice AI, Phone System and Agent
#   Studio. Those tool names live in the raw/legacy surface, so a `curated` box
#   CANNOT serve them. If the fleet needs them, use `stable` (curated +
#   official + live-docs-supplemental + legacy-compatible) rather than `full`.
#   Decide this deliberately — it is a capability decision, not a tuning knob.

# The tool the install smoke test and QC call to prove Tier 2 returns real data.
# It MUST exist inside the configured profile.
#   curated              -> crm_list_workspaces   (verified present)
#   stable/raw/full      -> ghl_list_products     (the classic Tier 2 smoke test)
GHL_MCP_SMOKE_TOOL="crm_list_workspaces"

# Sanity band for the probe: the profile actually took effect if the live tool
# count lands inside this band. Widen when changing the profile.
#   curated -> 1..200      full -> 500..2000
GHL_MCP_EXPECT_MIN_TOOLS="1"
GHL_MCP_EXPECT_MAX_TOOLS="200"

# Canonical port (8765 — 8000 is Cognee on most boxes).
GHL_MCP_PORT="8765"

# ── D6 (P0 SECURITY): LOOPBACK BIND ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The server holds the client's GHL Private Integration Token and serves
# GET /tools to anyone, UNAUTHENTICATED. Upstream binds every interface from a
# HARDCODED literal in BOTH entry points — src/main.ts:209 and
# src/http-server.ts:176, each `app.listen(port, '0.0.0.0', …)`. There is no
# HOST or MCP_SERVER_HOST variable in the pinned tree, so setting one in a plist
# or compose file changes NOTHING; it only looks like a fix.
#
# This value is therefore consumed by OUR generated .ghl-mcp-bind-guard.cjs,
# which the launcher preloads with `node --require` and which rewrites the host
# argument of net.Server.prototype.listen. It deliberately lives OUTSIDE dist/:
# the installer rebuilds dist/ from the pinned commit and swaps it on every run,
# so anything patched into dist/ is reverted on the next build on every box.
# Proven end-to-end by tests/unit/ghl-mcp-bind-guard.test.sh.
#
# A fleet survey on 2026-08-03 found 19 Mac client boxes listening on *:8765
# with /tools answering 200 unauthenticated.
GHL_MCP_BIND_HOST="127.0.0.1"

# ── SUPPLY CHAIN: bind the dependency tree, not just the source ──────────────
# The commit pin fixes the SOURCE; it says nothing about what npm resolves. The
# build refuses when package-lock.json at the pinned commit does not hash to
# this value, so a tampered or drifted lockfile cannot be installed under a
# vetted commit. Recompute on every pin bump:
#   git -C <clone> archive <PIN> | tar -xO package-lock.json | shasum -a 256
# Measured at bfc2bbe on 2026-08-03; `npm ci --ignore-scripts` against it
# installs 415 packages and the build then yields a dist/main.js containing
# connect(transport).
GHL_MCP_DEPS_LOCK_SHA256="654b6d34141a624f2f2e75b09dc1786d437ba7f15eed0167ca1f467abe6b9131"

# LOG ROTATION. Nothing in the fleet ever rotated this server's logs: the
# operator box's ghl-mcp/stderr.log reached 5.4 MB and a second fleet box's
# 2.2 MB, both growing since May. Rotation is copytruncate-style (copy then
# truncate IN
# PLACE) because launchd/pm2 hold an open fd on the file — renaming it would
# leave the server writing to an orphaned inode and the visible log frozen.
GHL_MCP_LOG_MAX_BYTES="10485760"   # rotate at 10 MB
GHL_MCP_LOG_KEEP="3"               # keep .1 .2 .3 -> ~40 MB worst case per stream

# Seconds the probe waits for a JSON-RPC response before declaring the server
# DEAF (alive, listening, and answering nothing).
GHL_MCP_PROBE_TIMEOUT="10"

# ── THE SOURCE: AN ORG-CONTROLLED MIRROR, NOT UPSTREAM ──────────────────────
# Kept here so no script hardcodes a second copy. It is also BOUND INTO THE
# VETTING DIGEST (tuple field 7), because a SHA names an OBJECT and never the
# host that serves it: with the URL unbound, this could be repointed at an
# attacker's clone while the commit, the verdict and the digest all still
# checked out, and every byte the box executes would have changed.
#
# WHY A MIRROR AT ALL. Upstream force-pushes rewritten history (it carries an
# automated codex/daily-ghl-api-refresh branch) and publishes ZERO tags and ZERO
# releases. The pin resolved only because it happened to be upstream main's tip.
# When main moves past it the object is garbage-collected: boxes with an
# existing clone survive on their local copy, but `git fetch origin <sha>` from
# a FRESH clone fails and every new provisioning breaks permanently with
# PIN_MISMATCH. Pinning makes a dependency reproducible; MIRRORING is what makes
# the pin durable. The mirror's main is a byte-identical copy of upstream so
# commit SHAs still cross-reference against upstream (that is how a tampered
# third-party tree is detected), and a ruleset blocks force-push and deletion.
#
# WHY THE PIN IS A MIRROR-LOCAL COMMIT. The mirror also CARRIES A SECURITY
# PATCH, on branch openclaw-patched. Upstream hardcodes 0.0.0.0 in BOTH HTTP
# entry points with no variable to change it, serves GET /tools unauthenticated,
# and answers a disallowed Origin with 500 instead of the spec-required 403.
# Because this installer rebuilds dist/ from the pinned SOURCE on every run, a
# dist-level fix is destroyed by the next build — so the fix has to live in
# source, on a branch. openclaw-patch-01 adds GHL_MCP_BIND_HOST (default
# 127.0.0.1), Origin validation returning 403, and an opt-in bearer gate. See
# PATCHES.md on that branch for every divergence and the upstream PR that would
# retire it.
#
# This does NOT make GHL_MCP_BIND_HOST's bind-guard shim redundant. The shim
# still runs, and must: it is what protects a box whose pin is ever moved back
# to an unpatched commit, and belt-and-braces on a credentialed endpoint is not
# waste. Two independent mechanisms, one outcome.
#
# Existing clones are migrated automatically: ensure_repo_at_pin (Mac) and
# pin_mcp_checkout (VPS) repoint `origin` before fetching, so a box provisioned
# against upstream moves to the mirror on its next run.
GHL_MCP_REPO_URL="https://github.com/trevorotts1/ghl-community-mcp-mirror.git"
